<header><p class="enc-kicker">Religion / idea · religion</p><h1 class="enc-title">Sufriyya</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Sufrites, Sufrism, Sufris</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Sufriyya were a moderate branch of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kharijites">Kharijites</a>, the early Islamic movement that rejected both Ali and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/umayyad-caliphate">Umayyads</a>, which emerged in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iraq">Iraq</a> in the late 7th century. Spread by missionaries to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/north-africa">North Africa</a>, it inspired the great <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/berbers">Berber</a> revolt of 740 against the Umayyad <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caliphate">Caliphate</a>, and Sufri Berber states arose at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tlemcen">Tlemcen</a> under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/abu-qurra">Abu Qurra</a> and at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sijilmasa">Sijilmasa</a> under the Midrarids. The movement faded by the 11th century.</p></div>
